"slayer88" <slayergwarironmai...@yahoo.com> wrote:

> I'm new to nethack and I am addicted to it.  Does
> anyone know what the all around best class is?

That depends on your intentions.

If you are going to obsess on winning the game for
the first time as soon as possible, by all means
play Valkeries, their ascent rates excel.

Some of those here have played for decades, yet
never ascended, so there are other options.

If you are instead playing the game purely for fun,
play _all_ the class/race/alignment combinations, at
random, letting the game choose them for you. Each
has things to teach, and the variety keeps the game
new.

If you want to make the game as hard as possible,
healers and tourists are the way to go until you
conquer their shortfalls, then spread out to the
other classes.

      [Myself, I play orcish wizards or human
      healers almost exclusively, for complex
      reasons spelled "f" "u" "n".]

Another piece of unsolicited advice: it isn't at all
obvious, but NetHack is _designed_ top to bottom to
be a _puzzle_, where discovering what things do,
what objectives to set, what possessions are best,
purely by playing the game, is a huge part of the
entertainment possibilities.

In that regard, it is like Zork or Adventure or
Bard's Tale, not like more modern games.

If you hang around this newsgroup picking up
tidbits, or consult the huge set of available online
"spoiler" documents, almost all of that aspect of
the game will be irrecoverably lost to you, and it
will become much more mechanical, which might make
it lots less fun.

The choice is entirely yours.

A second piece of unsolicited advice: better players
learn, over time, to treat NetHack like chess, not
like Doom.

That means to contemplate each move, all the visible
board, all the available status information, all the
accessible resources carried or seen, the
conformation of the rooms and hallways within
running distance, the locations and status of your
pets, the enemy, and the peaceful monsters, and the
recent messages, at length before making that single
next move.

You _can_ make more than one move per second. You
are _advised_ to make no more than one move per
couple of minutes.

NetHack has no between moves timer, no need for good
twitch reflexes, no reason to rush to make the next
one move.

That's a _very_ hard lesson to learn.

        [I've been playing since 1987, still haven't
